                <pb facs="0887" n="817" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg817"/>

                <head>No. 523.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Heap
                        to Mr. Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-12-03"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 3, 1882</date>.
                        (Received December 22.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 148.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>:* * * In reference to the case of Dr.
                    Frederick L. Kingsbury, mentioned in No. 33, dated the 16th of November last, I
                    have been informed, both by himself and Mr. Wyndham, the charge d’affaires of
                    Great Britain, that his difficulties with the Bulgarian authorities have been
                    satisfactorily arranged, and he has received permission to practice his
                    profession in the principality. This settlement has been effected through the
                    good offices of Mr. Lascelles, Her Majesty’s agent in Bulgaria, acting under
                    instructions from the Earl of Dufferin. I have requested Mr. Wyndham to convey
                    the thanks of this legation to Mr. Lascelles.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>G. H. HEAP.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
